.TH CLEAR-CLAUDE 1 "2026-10-26" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
Clear-Claude \- clear the Claude Code conversation a schedule runs, removing
every local record of it, and keeping the schedule and its work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Clear-Claude
.RB [ \-C | \-\-directory
.IR DIRECTORY ]
.BR \-s | \-\-schedule
.I SCHEDULE
.RB [ \-\-clear " | " \-\-retire ]
.br
.B Clear-Claude
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Clear-Claude
clears the Claude Code conversation that
.BR Start-Claude (1)
runs for a schedule, removing from this computer every record of it:
.IP \(bu 2
the transcript, and the folder of tool results and subagent transcripts beside it;
.IP \(bu 2
the file history: copies of files as they were before the conversation edited them;
.IP \(bu 2
the session environment;
.IP \(bu 2
the conversation's temporary output under
.IR /tmp/claude-<uid> ;
.IP \(bu 2
the memory files the conversation wrote, found by the
.B originSessionId
each carries, and their lines in the memory index;
.IP \(bu 2
the prompts typed in the conversation, from the prompt history.
.PP
It reads the schedule's background session from Start-Claude's store, and
holds the store's lock for the schedule throughout, so that no launch resumes
or renames the conversation while it is removed.
.SS With \-\-clear, the default
The background session and every connection to it go on, in a new
conversation with nothing of the old one to read. Nothing is removed before
the new conversation is confirmed. First the memories the conversation wrote,
their lines in the memory index and the job folder's scratch files are moved,
not removed, into a staging folder of the run's own under the job folder, with
a manifest naming each entry's original path: moved, since a new conversation
reads the memory index as it starts and would find the scratch. Then
.B /clear
is typed into a viewer of the script's own, a tmux session no client attaches:
the text alone, and Enter a second after the viewer's input line shows it,
since claude takes an Enter sent with the text as part of the command. While
the input line still shows the command, Enter is pressed again, up to three
times in all. Once claude lists a new conversation for the background
session, the viewer closes, the old conversation's records go, the staged
entries with them, and its prompts leave the prompt history.
.PP
When the viewer cannot open, or
.B /clear
is not taken, each staged entry goes back: one whose original path exists
again, written meanwhile by another session or the conversation itself, is
left staged and named, never overwritten, and an index line goes back only
where the index has no line for its memory. When
.B /clear
was typed and claude's list then cannot be read, the outcome is unknown:
nothing goes back, nothing is removed, the staging folder is named, and the
command exits 75 for the owner to settle. A staging folder an interrupted run
left is named by the next run and not touched.
.PP
Every viewer, tmux pane, ssh or mosh session, sbx exec and Remote Control
stays connected throughout.
.SS With \-\-retire
The background session goes too. Every viewer ends first: each is a
.B "claude attach <short id>"
process where claude runs, and ending it ends whatever stands in front of it,
a tmux pane, an ssh or mosh session, or sbx exec, live or dead, wherever its
terminal is. The session is stopped and removed with
.B "claude stop"
and
.BR "claude rm" ,
its records go, and so do its job folder and the schedule's entry in the
store, so that the next
.B "Start-Claude \-\-schedule"
starts a new conversation. Last, it asks claude's list again, and succeeds
only on a valid list with the session absent: a session listed again was
restarted by a connection, and a list that fails or cannot be read is a
failure of its own. Remote Control is the one connection it cannot end:
disconnect it on claude.ai first.
.PP
Either way, a memory is matched on the
.B originSessionId
field of its frontmatter and the whole id, never on a mention in its body;
only a memory folder the run itself emptied is removed; and every path it
removes or stages is resolved first and must lie under
.I CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
or
.IR /tmp/claude-<uid> :
a project entry that is a symlink, or any path leading out of that tree, is
skipped and named.
.PP
Either way it keeps the schedule itself, its worktrees and branches,
everything on GitHub, other conversations, and the directory's entry in
.IR ~/.claude.json ,
which keeps the directory trusted. It removes nothing Anthropic holds; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
A schedule with no entry in the store has nothing to remove, and the command
exits 0.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-schedule " " \fISCHEDULE\fR
The schedule:
.IR <owner>/<repository>#<number> ,
or a bare
.I <number>
in the directory's repository. Required.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-directory " " \fIDIRECTORY\fR
The root of a repository clone, or of a linked worktree, resolved to its
physical path. It names a bare number's repository. Default: the current
directory.
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Clear the conversation, keeping the background session and every connection to
it. The default.
.TP
.B \-\-retire
Remove the background session too, ending every connection to it. Refused with
.BR \-\-clear .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show this page.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-~/.local/state}/agent-schedule/<owner>/<repository>/<number>
Start-Claude's store entry: the background session's short id, beside its lock.
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:\-~/.claude}/projects/<project>/<session>.jsonl
The transcript, beside its folder
.IR <session>/ ,
and the project's
.IR memory/ .
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:\-~/.claude}/file-history/<session>
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:\-~/.claude}/session-env/<session>
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:\-~/.claude}/jobs/<short id>
The background session's job folder. When clearing, its scratch files,
.IR tmp/ ,
are staged in
.IR clear-<time>-<pid>/ ,
whose
.I manifest.tsv
names each staged entry's original path, and the staging folder goes once the
clear is confirmed, or stays when the outcome is unknown; when retiring, the
job folder is removed.
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:\-~/.claude}/history.jsonl
The prompt history, one line per prompt, each carrying its conversation's id;
see
.BR NOTES .
Its lock,
.IR history.jsonl.clear-claude.lock ,
a directory, serializes Clear-Claude runs.
.TP
.I /tmp/claude-<uid>/<project>/<session>
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The conversation was removed, or the store held none for the schedule.
.TP
.B 64
A usage error: no
.BR \-\-schedule ,
one not in either form, or
.B \-\-clear
given with
.BR \-\-retire .
.TP
.B 65
The store's entry holds no session id; or claude's list is not what this
script reads: no array, two background entries for the id, or a conversation
id that is not a UUID.
.TP
.B 66
No such directory, not the root of a repository clone, no such repository on
GitHub, or, when clearing, no background session to clear.
.TP
.B 69
claude cannot list its sessions; tmux opened no viewer;
.B /clear
was not taken, or started no new conversation, and every staged entry went
back; or claude did not remove the background session.
.TP
.B 74
The prompt history could not be read or rewritten. Before the rename it is
untouched; after it, the lines appended meanwhile are saved beside it, in
.IR history.jsonl.clear-claude.unmerged .
Everything else the run was to remove is gone.
.TP
.B 75
It was run inside the conversation it removes; another launch held the
store's lock for 10 seconds; another Clear-Claude held the prompt history's
lock for 30 seconds; when clearing,
.B /clear
was typed and claude's list then could not be read, so the outcome is
unknown and the staged entries are kept in the folder named; or, when
retiring, a connection restarted the background session after it stopped.
Disconnect Remote Control, and run it again from a terminal outside the
conversation.
.SH NOTES
Records are local to the computer claude runs on: run it there.
.PP
When clearing, the background session's timeline,
.IR jobs/<short\ id>/timeline.jsonl ,
is left: the running session keeps it.
.PP
The prompt history is shared: every claude session on the host appends to it,
and no lock they honor covers it. The old conversation's lines are taken out
by a rewrite that keeps the old file open, writes a copy without them beside
it, renames the copy into place, and appends whatever reached the old file
meanwhile, read through the kept descriptor, until it has stopped growing for
two seconds. The rewrite is not lossless: a session that opened the old file
before the rename and writes more than two seconds later loses that line, and
nothing bounds that delay. In practice a session opens, appends and closes in
one go.
.PP
What a conversation sent to Anthropic stays with Anthropic. A Remote Control
session is deleted from claude.ai/code; a report sent with
.B /feedback
is kept for five years; deletion of the rest is requested from
privacy@anthropic.com.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR Start-Claude (1),
.BR claude (1)
